      "writtenOn": "2024-02-26T17:13:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The suggestion does not work exactly, because `STM32_USART_CR1_FIFOEN` is not a `CONFIG_xxx`-type define from `board.h`, but rather a bitfield which may be defined in some `registers-stm32xxx.h` files (currently only `registers-stm32l5.h`).\n\nOther drivers, which need to differ by chip family will typically do something like `#if defined(CHIP_FAMILY_STM32F0) || defined(CHIP_FAMILY_STM32L4)`.  I feel that it can make sense to check the register bit field directly, rather than having to list each of the various variants which have support for that bit.\n\nIn this case, if the hardware supports FIFO, I do not see any disadvantage to using it (8-byte FIFO instead of effectively one-byte FIFO), which is why I found no need to introduce a `CONFIG_xxx` setting.",
      "parentUuid": "9b909e79_5a87724a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "60b11fc1aaf071042388117308ebf79fa9c3585f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fe50b3c_ed127932",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/usart_tx_interrupt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-26T21:05:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for consideration. I\u0027ll leave it to you.\n \nI\u0027m concerned using STM32_USART_CR1_FIFOEN as a compile swtich as well as a bit mask leads to confusion. So, it looks cleaner to me to guard the sections by IS_ENABLED(CHIP_FAMILY_STM32L5). Bit masks tend to be defined whether they exist in the chip or not (in most directories of this codebase, IIUC). chip/stm32 is a bit different in that regards.",
      "parentUuid": "07fe0397_fa2684ec",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "60b11fc1aaf071042388117308ebf79fa9c3585f",
